022,"team":"IND","distance":0.4395,"shared":4,"play_caller":null},{"season":2022,"team":"TEN","distance":0.4496,"shared":4,"play_caller":null}]},"overview":{"overview":"The Bengals are 5-2 through week 8 of the 2026 season and have outscored opponents 206 to 158. The offense has been carrying the team, ranking 5th in EPA per play while the defense sits 15th in EPA allowed. Their most distinctive traits are the highest pass rate over expected and the fewest pass rushers in the league.","offense":"The Bengals offense has been one of the best in the league by EPA per play so far, and it has been much better passing the ball than running it. They have thrown more than almost anyone relative to the situation, even when the score has not called for it. Their biggest outliers in how they line up are the 2nd highest shotgun rate and the 2nd lowest under center rate in the league. Joe Burrow has run the passing game, with Tee Higgins leading the team in targets.","defense":"The Bengals defense has been about average by EPA allowed per play so far. Its strongest area has been sack rate, where it ranks 4th, and its weakest has been explosive play rate allowed, where it ranks 29th. Boye Mafe leads the team with 5.5 sacks, and Dexter Lawrence has added 5, after not being a regular on this defense last season. In terms of scheme, they have the fewest pass rushers in the league.","special":"The Bengals have been one of the most conservative teams on fourth down, going for it on only 17% of their short-yardage chances. Their kickers have made 100% of field goals, which is the best in the league. On the roster side, they put an unusually large share of their salary cap into receivers, about 22% of the money on the books. Their quarterbacks were among the oldest in the league.","players":"Joe Burrow has played nearly every snap at quarterback and thrown for 2,115 yards. Tee Higgins has been the clear top target with 23% of the looks and 726 receiving yards. Chase Brown has handled most of the work in the backfield with 71% of the carries. On defense, Boye Mafe leads the team with 5.5 sacks and Jordan Battle has picked off 3 passes.","coaching":"Zac Taylor is calling the offense as head coach and Al Golden is calling the defense. Their offense looks most like the 2023 Commanders and the 2025 Chiefs under Andy Reid. Their defense looks most like the 2023 Cardinals under Nick Rallis and the 2025 Rams under Chris Shula.","trends":"The offense went from 19th in EPA per play in 2025 to 5th so far this season, a big step forward. The defense moved from 29th in EPA allowed per play in 2025 to 15th so far this season, a big step forward.","notes":"The 2026 pages are built from data that nflverse updates every week, so they will keep changing until the season ends. Personnel, formations, coverage and pressure numbers are missing for now because that data is only released after the season."}}